Create encrypted backup of .env

Usage: evnx backup [OPTIONS] [ENV]

Arguments:
  [ENV]
          Path to the .env file to back up

Options:
      --env-name <NAME>
          Back up `.env.<NAME>` instead.
          
          `--env-name production` resolves to `.env.production`. A name whose file does not exist is an error listing the environments that do.

      --output <OUTPUT>
          Destination path for the encrypted backup (default: <env>.backup)

      --password-file <PATH>
          Path to a file holding the encryption password.
          
          Enables non-interactive / CI usage. The file's contents are read and fed into Argon2id in place of a typed password. UTF-8 content is used with leading and trailing whitespace trimmed; binary content is Base64-encoded first. It must come to at least 8 characters, as a typed passphrase must.
          
          `evnx restore` takes the same flag, so one file round-trips. EVNX_PASSWORD is honoured too, at lower priority, so a scheduled backup needs no passphrase on disk; when both are set, the file wins and a warning says so.
          
          Examples: evnx backup --password-file /run/secrets/evnx-pass EVNX_PASSWORD=$PASS evnx backup

      --keep <N>
          Number of previous backups to retain alongside the new one.
          
          Once the new backup is written, existing ones shift up one place: <output> → <output>.1 → … → <output>.N. The one already at <output>.N is replaced by the shift (with a warning); files past it are never touched. Set to 0 to disable rotation (overwrite silently). Defaults to `3`, or `[backup] keep` from `.evnx.toml`.

      --verify
          Re-decrypt the backup after writing and verify content integrity.
          
          Costs one more Argon2id round (a fraction of a second to about a second, depending on the machine) but proves the backup is readable before you discard the source file. Exit code 6 on failure.

      --no-color
          Disable colored output

  -q, --quiet
          Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print

  -v, --verbose
          Enable verbose output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Passphrase: --password-file, then EVNX_PASSWORD, then a prompt (asked twice). A new
backup needs at least 8 characters from any of them.

Exit codes:
  0  Backup written (and verified, with --verify)
  2  Could not run: source file, key file or passphrase (or the confirmation did
     not match), an output that is the source or a directory, encryption, writing
     the file, or the integrity check after it (--verify); the message says which

A backup is a file you keep, behind a passphrase you choose; it needs no account
and no network. To share and version secrets instead, see `evnx cloud push`.

📖  Full guide: https://docs.evnx.dev/cli/commands/backup
